      <article class="guide-card">
        <div class="guide-label">PipelineSync field guide</div>
        <h2>The practical framework</h2>
        <p class="intro">A CRM architecture is more than a collection of properties and pipelines. It is the operating model behind your go-to-market motion. This blueprint gives you a practical sequence for designing HubSpot so teams can trust the data, managers can trust the forecast, and future changes do not create another cleanup project.</p>
        <section><h3>Start with the revenue motion</h3><p>Before creating properties, map how a person moves from first touch to customer and expansion. Separate the business questions you need to answer from the tools you use to answer them. This prevents the portal from becoming a mirror of every historical process and gives you a model that can support change.</p><ul><li>Document the lifecycle from anonymous visitor through customer and evangelist.</li><li>Define which objects represent people, companies, deals, tickets, and custom business entities.</li><li>Write the decision rules that move a record from one stage to the next.</li></ul></section><section><h3>Design objects, pipelines, and stages together</h3><p>Objects describe what you manage; pipelines describe the process; stages describe progress through that process. Keep those layers distinct. A deal stage should represent a verifiable buyer or delivery milestone, not an internal task such as “send proposal.”</p><ul><li>Use one source of truth for each business concept.</li><li>Keep stage names short, observable, and tied to exit criteria.</li><li>Use separate pipelines only when the process, ownership, or reporting logic is genuinely different.</li></ul></section><section><h3>Permissions and governance are part of the build</h3><p>A scalable portal makes the safe path the easy path. Create permission sets around roles, define who can edit sensitive properties, and publish naming conventions before teams start creating their own fields and workflows.</p><ul><li>Create a property catalogue with owner, definition, data type, and allowed values.</li><li>Set rules for required fields, sensitive data, and export access.</li><li>Review unused properties, workflows, and views on a regular cadence.</li></ul></section>
        <section>
          <h3>Implementation checklist</h3>
          <p>Use this checklist to turn the framework into a working next step.</p>
          <div class="guide-checklist"><div class="guide-check"><span>✓</span><div>Lifecycle definitions approved</div></div><div class="guide-check"><span>✓</span><div>Pipeline exit criteria documented</div></div><div class="guide-check"><span>✓</span><div>Property catalogue assigned</div></div><div class="guide-check"><span>✓</span><div>Permission sets tested</div></div><div class="guide-check"><span>✓</span><div>Reporting requirements mapped</div></div><div class="guide-check"><span>✓</span><div>Change-request process published</div></div></div>
        </section>
        <div class="guide-callout"><p><strong>Key takeaway:</strong> The best architecture is the one your team can explain in one page. Start with the decisions the business needs to make, then configure HubSpot to make those decisions visible.</p></div>
